From 4dbfc501277b627c55409f1db256aa90131f7e4b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zack Garza <dzackgarza@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:07:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Formalize divisibility in a Gram-matrix lattice, and the
 twist that fixes the Coble lemma
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`divis G v` is the gcd of the values of the form `G` at `v` over a basis of `ℤⁿ`.
The file proves the two algebraic facts the Coble cusp computations spend, in
general rather than at the toy matrix they first appeared with:

* `divis_twist`: twisting the form by `c` multiplies every divisibility by `|c|`,
  so `divis (2 • G) v = 2 * divis G v` (`divis_two_smul`);
* hence every divisibility in `T_Co = L(2)` is even (`two_dvd_divis_two_smul`),
  and `divis (2 • G) v = 2` exactly when `divis G v = 1`
  (`divis_two_smul_eq_two_iff`).

The counterexamples stay, because they are why the statement has the form it has:
in `⟨2⟩` the generator has divisibility 2, its double has 4, and its triple has 6,
so neither "pairs nontrivially" nor "is not a double" isolates divisibility 2.

Not formalized, and the docstring says so: that `divis G v = 1` is primitivity
uses unimodularity of `G`, and that a lattice identification `T_Co = L(2)` is a
statement about lattices rather than about Gram matrices.

The subtree is separate from `LeanCategories.Lattices`, which holds Mathlib's
order-theoretic lattices, and is imported from the root module so a default build
covers it.

