From ea9efa47cfa4d87bf69df986db3087476d299f68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zack Garza <dzackgarza@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:32:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Prove the disputed theorem through the integral duality
 isomorphism, not through a gcd
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The theorem is `divis (2 • G) v = 2 ↔ Primitive v` for an integral unimodular `G`, where
`Primitive v` says that no `m > 1` has `v = m • u`. Hypotheses stated in full: `G` is a
Gram matrix over `ℤ` and `IsUnit G.det`, that is `det G = ±1`; `v` is any vector of `ℤⁿ`;
the form whose divisibility is computed is `2 • G`.

The route is now the manuscript's, whose disputed step was "`β_L(v,-)` is divisible by
`m` exactly when `v ∈ mL`":

* `form_single`, `form_apply_eq_sum`: the values of the form are the coordinates of `Gᵀv`
  combined with the coordinates of the argument;
* `forall_dvd_form_iff`: **the duality step** — for unimodular `G`, `m` divides every
  value of `β_G(v,-)` exactly when `v ∈ mL`. The content is the forward direction: the
  dual vector attached to `v` is `m` times an integral one, and the inverse of the duality
  isomorphism is the adjugate over the determinant, integral because `det G` is a unit;
* `divis_dvd_form`, `divis_eq_one_iff_forall_not_dvd`: divisibility one is indivisibility
  of the functional, no `m > 1` dividing all its values;
* `divis_two_smul_eq_two_iff_primitive`: the theorem, both halves being the two halves of
  the manuscript's sentence.

`Primitive` is defined coordinate-free and `primitive_iff_isPrimitive` identifies it with
the gcd formulation of `IsPrimitive`, so the earlier gcd-based theorem
