From 4908974de010dc8ed711c6decbb504eb92b320c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zack Garza <dzackgarza@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:36:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Formalize the stable-group separation of isotropic lines,
 on the small core
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The manuscript's cusp lemma says the stable orthogonal group has at least two orbits on
the primitive isotropic vectors of `T_Co`, and the whole of that claim is a separation of
two lines. Its proof needs no lattice API: an automorphism acting trivially modulo `2`
cannot send one residue class to another, because line equality forces the image to be
`± f` and the two signs agree modulo `2`.

* `ActsTriviallyModTwo g := ∀ x, ∃ y, g x = x + 2 • y` — the hypothesis, which is what
  "lies in the discriminant kernel" states for `T = B(2)` with `A_T ≅ B/2B`;
* `sub_mem_two_of_span_eq`: if `g` acts trivially modulo `2`, `f ≠ 0`, and
  `span {g e} = span {f}`, then `e - f ∈ 2 • ⊤`. Equality of the two spans gives
  `g e = m • f` and `f = k • (g e)`, so `f = (k * m) • f` and `k * m = 1` by
  torsion-freeness; hence `m = ± 1` and the two cases give `e - f = 2 • (-y)` and
  `e - f = 2 • (e + y)` respectively;
* `span_ne_span_of_distinct_residue`: the contrapositive, the form the manuscript uses;
* `span_single_ne_span_single`: the counterexample — for `i ≠ j` no such automorphism
  carries `span {eᵢ}` to `span {eⱼ}`. In the Coble lattice `e` and `f` are the isotropic
  generators of the `U(2)` summand, which are basis vectors of `B` with distinct residues.

What this proves is exactly the separation, which is what the counterexample needs: at
least two orbits, so at least two `0`-cusps. It is not a classification of the orbits of
`Γ_Co`, and the count of those remains open in the manuscript.

Differences from the manuscript's presentation, recorded rather than papered over: the
formal statement needs only a `ℤ`-linear automorphism, not an isometry, so it applies to
the stable subgroup a fortiori; and it obtains `g e = ± f` from equality of spans and
`f ≠ 0` rather than from primitivity of `e`, which the manuscript's version uses.

Axioms: `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`, no `sorryAx`. Build green under the
4.33.0 pin, with the divisibility theorem untouched.

